  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-11" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Where to Eat in Bruges</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=De+Gastro,+Braambergstraat+6,+8000+Brugge,+Belgium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>De Gastro</a> – A favourite of mine in Bruges, this restaurant is perfect for trying traditional Belgian cuisine with quality ingredients in a cosy and elegant setting.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=ONE+Restaurant,+Arsenaalstraat+55,+8000+Brugge,+Belgium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>One Restaurant</a> – A great choice for a special dinner, offering a refined take on Belgian cuisine with seasonal ingredients and a modern touch.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=Cambrinus,+Philipstockstraat+19,+8000+Brugge,+Belgium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>Cambrinus</a> – A classic Bruges spot for authentic Belgian dishes, famous for its traditional food and impressive selection of local beers.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=The+Republic,+Sint-Jakobsstraat+36,+8000+Brugge,+Belgium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>The Republic</a> – A relaxed local favourite with a cosy atmosphere, ideal for lunch, drinks or a casual meal away from the busiest tourist spots.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=That%27s+Toast,+Dweersstraat+4,+8000+Brugge,+Belgium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>That's Toast</a> – One of the best brunch places in Bruges, serving delicious breakfast plates, coffee and a welcoming atmosphere to start your day.</p>
    <p><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=MARO,+Westmeers+9,+8000+Brugge,+Belgium" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={mapLink}>MARO</a> – A stylish café perfect for coffee, brunch or a light lunch, combining fresh dishes with a cosy and modern vibe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-12" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">FAQ</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={`${prose} space-y-6`}>
    <div id="faq-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">1. What Is the Best Time to Visit Bruges?</h3>
      <p>Bruges is a beautiful destination year-round, and the best time to visit really depends on the kind of experience you’re looking for. Spring (April–June) and early autumn (September–October) are great if you prefer pleasant weather and fewer crowds, while winter brings a completely different atmosphere, with festive lights and Christmas decorations making the city feel even more magical.</p>
      <p>I visited Bruges during the Christmas season, and I loved seeing the city at this time of year. If you’re planning a winter trip, Bruges at Christmas is something I’d happily recommend — especially if you enjoy festive markets, beautiful decorations, and a cozy atmosphere.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">2. How Many Days Do You Need in Bruges?</h3>
      <p>Many travelers choose to spend just one day in Bruges, and it is absolutely possible to see the city’s main highlights in a single day. Most of the places I’ve mentioned in this guide — from Markt Square and the Belfry to Rozenhoedkaai, Boniface Bridge, the canals, and Burg Square — are close enough to explore on foot, so you can fit a lot into one day.</p>
      <p>Personally, though, I’d recommend staying for a weekend in Bruges if you have the time. Two days give you the chance to see the main sights while also experiencing the city at a slower pace, especially in the early morning and evening when the day-trippers have left. And if you’re traveling as a couple, Bruges makes a lovely destination for a romantic weekend, with its canals, beautiful architecture, cozy restaurants, and charming streets.</p>
      <p>If you’re short on time, don’t worry — one day in Bruges is enough to see a lot. But if you can stay a little longer, I think you’ll appreciate the city even more.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">3. What Should I Not Miss in Bruges?</h3>
      <p>If you’re still wondering what you really shouldn’t miss in Bruges, I’d narrow it down to three places: Markt Square, Rozenhoedkaai, and Boniface Bridge.</p>
      <p>Markt Square gives you that classic Bruges experience, with its colorful historic buildings and the iconic Belfry. Rozenhoedkaai is the canal view you’ll probably recognize from photos of Bruges, while Boniface Bridge was one of my personal favorites, especially around sunrise.</p>
      <p>Of course, there’s much more to see and do in Bruges, but if you’re short on time and can only choose three, these would be my picks.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h3 class="mb-2 text-black">4. What Is Bruges Famous For?</h3>
      <p>Bruges isn’t famous for just one thing. It’s best known for its beautiful medieval architecture, which makes you feel like you’ve stepped back in time, its picturesque canals — one of the reasons it’s often called the Venice of the North — and, of course, its Belgian waffles and chocolate. It’s hard to leave this beautiful city without taking home a box of Belgian chocolates, especially with so many tempting chocolate shops around the city. Bruges is also home to the iconic Belfry Tower overlooking Markt Square, and Belgian beer is another local specialty you’ll find throughout the city.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

